1
00:00:00,102 --> 00:00:04,239
In 1868, the richest man in America lost a
war.

2
00:00:05,039 --> 00:00:06,845
He was seventy-four years old.

3
00:00:07,643 --> 00:00:11,845
He owned steamships and railroads, and he
almost never lost a fight.

4
00:00:12,643 --> 00:00:18,590
But three men on Wall Street beat him in
broad daylight, using a printing press and
a judge they had bought.

5
00:00:19,388 --> 00:00:20,686
He was furious.

6
00:00:21,484 --> 00:00:24,332
And what he did next did not just protect
his fortune.

7
00:00:25,130 --> 00:00:28,316
It built the most powerful railroad empire
in the world.

8
00:00:29,115 --> 00:00:32,639
This is the story of Cornelius Vanderbilt,
the Commodore.

9
00:00:33,438 --> 00:00:36,715
A boy who left school at eleven and could
barely spell.

10
00:00:37,513 --> 00:00:42,769
A man whose fortune, by some accounts,
rivaled the cash in the United States
Treasury.

11
00:00:43,568 --> 00:00:47,418
And a name that would lose every last
dollar of it within a hundred years.

12
00:00:48,216 --> 00:00:49,735
Stay until the end.

13
00:00:50,534 --> 00:00:53,004
Because the real twist is not how he won.

14
00:00:53,802 --> 00:00:55,413
It is what he left behind.

15
00:00:56,334 --> 00:01:03,205
Cornelius Vanderbilt was born on May
twenty-seventh, 1794, on Staten Island,
New York.

16
00:01:04,005 --> 00:01:05,512
His family was poor.

17
00:01:06,310 --> 00:01:11,840
Not starving poor, but the kind of poor
where every child worked and nobody had
time for school.

18
00:01:12,638 --> 00:01:15,017
Cornelius left the classroom at eleven.

19
00:01:15,815 --> 00:01:18,754
For the rest of his life, he wrote like a
child.

20
00:01:19,552 --> 00:01:21,150
His spelling was terrible.

21
00:01:21,948 --> 00:01:23,572
His grammar was worse.

22
00:01:24,370 --> 00:01:27,035
But he could see something educated men
missed.

23
00:01:27,834 --> 00:01:28,663
A bargain.

24
00:01:29,461 --> 00:01:30,447
A weakness.

25
00:01:31,245 --> 00:01:32,035
A moment.

26
00:01:32,834 --> 00:01:39,353
His father ferried vegetables and
passengers across the harbor to Manhattan,
and young Cornelius worked beside him.

27
00:01:40,151 --> 00:01:41,514
He learned the tides.

28
00:01:42,313 --> 00:01:43,650
He learned the winds.

29
00:01:44,448 --> 00:01:48,533
He learned which captains were lazy, and
which customers paid on time.

30
00:01:49,331 --> 00:01:51,892
By sixteen, he wanted his own boat.

31
00:01:52,690 --> 00:01:57,009
A small sailing boat cost about one
hundred dollars, and he had nothing.

32
00:01:57,808 --> 00:01:59,288
So he asked his mother.

33
00:02:00,086 --> 00:02:01,488
She made him an offer.

34
00:02:02,287 --> 00:02:08,611
If he cleared, plowed, and planted a rough
eight-acre field before his birthday, she
would lend him the money.

35
00:02:09,409 --> 00:02:11,527
Cornelius could not do that alone.

36
00:02:12,326 --> 00:02:15,147
So he did what he would do for the next
sixty years.

37
00:02:15,946 --> 00:02:17,048
He made a deal.

38
00:02:17,847 --> 00:02:24,184
According to the story, he promised his
friends rides on the new boat, and they
helped him finish the field on time.

39
00:02:24,982 --> 00:02:26,566
His mother kept her word.

40
00:02:27,365 --> 00:02:28,637
The boat was his.

41
00:02:29,435 --> 00:02:32,309
Within a year, he had paid back every
cent.

42
00:02:33,107 --> 00:02:38,220
At nineteen, he married his cousin Sophia,
and the two would have thirteen children.

43
00:02:39,018 --> 00:02:43,168
He was already running his own ferry
between Staten Island and Manhattan.

44
00:02:43,966 --> 00:02:48,415
The other sailors started calling him the
Commodore, and the name stuck for life.

45
00:02:49,214 --> 00:02:50,473
But he had a problem.

46
00:02:51,271 --> 00:02:54,301
In New York, the water belonged to someone
else.

47
00:02:55,230 --> 00:02:59,341
In the early 1800s, steam power was
changing everything.

48
00:03:00,141 --> 00:03:03,444
Robert Fulton had launched a steamboat on
the Hudson River.

49
00:03:04,243 --> 00:03:10,267
And the State of New York had given Fulton
and his partner a legal monopoly on steam
travel in its waters.

50
00:03:11,066 --> 00:03:14,655
Nobody else could run a steamboat there
without paying for permission.

51
00:03:15,454 --> 00:03:17,468
Most men saw a locked door.

52
00:03:18,266 --> 00:03:21,413
Vanderbilt saw a door that someone was
about to break.

53
00:03:22,211 --> 00:03:29,733
Around 1818, he went to work for a man
named Thomas Gibbons, who ran steamboats
between New Jersey and Manhattan,

54
00:03:29,933 --> 00:03:32,064
and who was fighting the monopoly in
court.

55
00:03:32,862 --> 00:03:37,077
Vanderbilt captained Gibbons's boat, and
he did it in open defiance.

56
00:03:37,875 --> 00:03:40,020
Monopoly agents tried to seize it.

57
00:03:40,818 --> 00:03:42,337
Vanderbilt dodged them.

58
00:03:43,136 --> 00:03:48,418
He bluffed, he sailed straight past the
officers, and the newspapers loved every
minute.

59
00:03:49,217 --> 00:03:50,619
Then came the case.

60
00:03:51,417 --> 00:03:56,478
In 1824, Gibbons versus Ogden reached the
United States Supreme Court.

61
00:03:57,276 --> 00:04:02,819
Chief Justice John Marshall ruled that
only the federal government could regulate
trade between the states.

62
00:04:03,618 --> 00:04:05,150
The monopoly collapsed.

63
00:04:05,948 --> 00:04:09,551
The waters were open to anyone with a boat
and the nerve to use it.

64
00:04:10,349 --> 00:04:11,830
Vanderbilt had both.

65
00:04:12,628 --> 00:04:18,041
He watched how Gibbons ran the business,
how he kept the books, how he managed the
crews.

66
00:04:18,839 --> 00:04:23,275
And in 1829, he started his own steamboat
line on the Hudson.

67
00:04:24,073 --> 00:04:26,413
His strategy fit in one sentence.

68
00:04:27,211 --> 00:04:29,108
Charge less than everyone else.

69
00:04:29,907 --> 00:04:33,926
He cut fares until his rivals were losing
money on every passenger.

70
00:04:34,724 --> 00:04:40,931
Prices to Albany fell so low that some
days, the story goes, he carried people
almost for free.

71
00:04:41,730 --> 00:04:44,577
Rival companies finally paid him to leave
the route.

72
00:04:45,375 --> 00:04:48,301
He took the money, and moved on to the
next one.

73
00:04:49,099 --> 00:04:49,759
Enter.

74
00:04:50,545 --> 00:04:51,374
Undercut.

75
00:04:52,159 --> 00:04:53,405
Get paid to leave.

76
00:04:54,204 --> 00:04:54,942
Repeat.

77
00:04:55,727 --> 00:04:59,486
Within a few years, the Commodore owned
dozens of steamboats.

78
00:05:00,284 --> 00:05:04,160
But the biggest opportunity of his life
had not even been discovered yet.

79
00:05:05,094 --> 00:05:08,593
In 1848, gold was found in California.

80
00:05:09,393 --> 00:05:13,959
By 1849, tens of thousands of people were
desperate to get there.

81
00:05:14,758 --> 00:05:16,225
The routes were terrible.

82
00:05:17,023 --> 00:05:20,431
Sail around the tip of South America,
which took months.

83
00:05:21,229 --> 00:05:27,253
Or cross the jungle of Panama, where one
company already controlled the traffic and
charged whatever it wanted.

84
00:05:28,052 --> 00:05:30,847
Vanderbilt looked at the map and saw a
third way.

85
00:05:31,646 --> 00:05:32,657
Nicaragua.

86
00:05:33,443 --> 00:05:39,519
His plan used the San Juan River, then
Lake Nicaragua, then a short road to the
Pacific coast.

87
00:05:40,318 --> 00:05:43,686
It cut days off the trip, and it could be
sold for less.

88
00:05:44,484 --> 00:05:50,326
In 1851, he formed the Accessory Transit
Company and built the whole thing from
scratch.

89
00:05:51,125 --> 00:05:52,527
Steamers on the river.

90
00:05:53,325 --> 00:05:54,506
Boats on the lake.

91
00:05:55,305 --> 00:05:57,553
Coaches over the last stretch of land.

92
00:05:58,351 --> 00:06:00,300
Then he did what he always did.

93
00:06:01,099 --> 00:06:02,892
He undercut the competition.

94
00:06:03,690 --> 00:06:05,092
Passengers poured in.

95
00:06:05,890 --> 00:06:07,475
The money poured in with them.

96
00:06:08,273 --> 00:06:13,699
By the early 1850s, Vanderbilt was
reported to be worth around eleven million
dollars,

97
00:06:13,898 --> 00:06:17,071
in a country where a skilled worker earned
about a dollar a day.

98
00:06:17,870 --> 00:06:19,754
He was fifty-nine years old.

99
00:06:20,552 --> 00:06:22,905
And he decided he deserved a vacation.

100
00:06:23,703 --> 00:06:30,522
So in 1853, he built a luxury steam yacht,
the North Star, nearly three hundred feet
long,

101
00:06:30,695 --> 00:06:34,089
with marble, velvet, and a private
orchestra on board.

102
00:06:34,888 --> 00:06:41,043
He packed his entire family on it and
sailed to Europe, the grandest private
voyage an American had ever made.

103
00:06:41,841 --> 00:06:47,540
He left his company in the hands of two
trusted partners, Charles Morgan and
Cornelius Garrison.

104
00:06:48,338 --> 00:06:52,227
It was the one time in his life that
Vanderbilt stopped watching his money.

105
00:06:53,026 --> 00:06:55,261
And it was a mistake he would never
forget.

106
00:06:56,190 --> 00:07:00,105
While Vanderbilt toured Europe, Morgan and
Garrison went to work.

107
00:07:00,906 --> 00:07:02,842
They maneuvered inside the company.

108
00:07:03,640 --> 00:07:09,365
They took control of the board, shifted
the money, and left the man who built the
route with almost nothing.

109
00:07:10,164 --> 00:07:15,303
When Vanderbilt came home, he found the
company he had created in somebody else's
hands.

110
00:07:16,101 --> 00:07:18,831
According to legend, he wrote them a short
letter.

111
00:07:19,630 --> 00:07:22,100
Gentlemen, you have undertaken to cheat
me.

112
00:07:22,898 --> 00:07:26,045
I will not sue you, for the law is too
slow.

113
00:07:26,843 --> 00:07:28,011
I will ruin you.

114
00:07:28,809 --> 00:07:32,764
Whether he wrote those exact words or not,
he did exactly that.

115
00:07:33,562 --> 00:07:39,261
Vanderbilt launched a rival line over the
same route and slashed fares to a fraction
of the old price.

116
00:07:40,059 --> 00:07:42,946
Passengers left Accessory Transit in
droves.

117
00:07:43,744 --> 00:07:45,212
Its stock collapsed.

118
00:07:46,010 --> 00:07:50,472
And Morgan and Garrison learned what every
rival of the Commodore eventually learned.

119
00:07:51,270 --> 00:07:52,503
He did not forget.

120
00:07:53,302 --> 00:07:54,795
And he did not forgive.

121
00:07:55,593 --> 00:07:58,779
The fight turned even uglier in Nicaragua
itself.

122
00:07:59,578 --> 00:08:05,212
An American adventurer named William
Walker had seized power there and sided
with Vanderbilt's enemies.

123
00:08:06,010 --> 00:08:08,063
Walker took over the transit route.

124
00:08:08,862 --> 00:08:14,769
Vanderbilt is said to have helped finance
the armies that drove Walker out, and by
1857,

125
00:08:14,968 --> 00:08:16,084
Walker was gone.

126
00:08:16,882 --> 00:08:24,521
Back on the ocean, the big shipping
companies were so tired of fighting him
that they started paying him every month
just to stay off their routes.

127
00:08:25,320 --> 00:08:30,863
And when the Civil War came, Vanderbilt
gave the Union his largest steamship, the
Vanderbilt,

128
00:08:30,958 --> 00:08:32,646
to hunt Confederate raiders.

129
00:08:33,445 --> 00:08:35,524
Congress thanked him with a gold medal.

130
00:08:36,322 --> 00:08:42,074
He had beaten his betrayers, outlasted
every rival at sea, and become a national
hero.

131
00:08:42,872 --> 00:08:44,040
And he was bored.

132
00:08:44,838 --> 00:08:48,037
Because out on the shore, he had noticed
something else.

133
00:08:48,835 --> 00:08:51,435
A new kind of machine that did not need
water.

134
00:08:52,374 --> 00:08:56,094
In the 1860s, American railroads were a
mess.

135
00:08:56,894 --> 00:08:58,609
Hundreds of small lines.

136
00:08:59,407 --> 00:09:00,549
Different tracks.

137
00:09:01,348 --> 00:09:02,554
Different schedules.

138
00:09:03,353 --> 00:09:07,437
Passengers changing trains again and
again, and paying every time.

139
00:09:08,236 --> 00:09:10,211
Vanderbilt was almost seventy.

140
00:09:11,009 --> 00:09:12,893
Most men that age retire.

141
00:09:13,691 --> 00:09:15,549
He started a second empire.

142
00:09:16,348 --> 00:09:20,302
He began buying the New York and Harlem
Railroad when its stock was cheap,

143
00:09:20,449 --> 00:09:22,698
trading for just a few dollars a share.

144
00:09:23,496 --> 00:09:30,224
The line ran through the heart of
Manhattan, and the right to run its cars
down the city streets came from the New
York City Council.

145
00:09:31,022 --> 00:09:33,909
Some of those council members found out he
was buying.

146
00:09:34,707 --> 00:09:36,682
And they saw a way to get rich.

147
00:09:37,480 --> 00:09:41,461
They would sell Harlem stock they did not
own, bet on the price falling,

148
00:09:41,621 --> 00:09:44,481
and then vote to take away the railroad's
street rights.

149
00:09:45,280 --> 00:09:46,812
The stock would crash.

150
00:09:47,611 --> 00:09:49,325
They would pocket the difference.

151
00:09:50,124 --> 00:09:51,630
It was a perfect plan.

152
00:09:52,428 --> 00:09:53,583
With one flaw.

153
00:09:54,381 --> 00:09:55,901
Vanderbilt knew about it.

154
00:09:56,699 --> 00:10:00,146
He and his allies quietly bought every
share they could find.

155
00:10:00,944 --> 00:10:04,768
And when the council members had to
deliver the stock they had promised to
sell,

156
00:10:04,980 --> 00:10:06,929
there was only one man who owned it.

157
00:10:07,728 --> 00:10:08,648
Vanderbilt.

158
00:10:09,434 --> 00:10:10,784
He named his price.

159
00:10:11,582 --> 00:10:13,557
They had no choice but to pay.

160
00:10:14,355 --> 00:10:18,856
Harlem stock climbed from single digits to
well over one hundred dollars a share,

161
00:10:19,069 --> 00:10:21,291
and the men who tried to trap him were
ruined.

162
00:10:22,090 --> 00:10:23,518
This is called a corner.

163
00:10:24,316 --> 00:10:27,593
And it made Vanderbilt the master of New
York's railroads.

164
00:10:28,392 --> 00:10:31,174
Next, he took the Hudson River Railroad.

165
00:10:31,973 --> 00:10:35,679
Then, in 1867, the New York Central.

166
00:10:36,478 --> 00:10:41,825
When the Central tried to cut his
passengers off, he reportedly stopped his
trains short of Albany,

167
00:10:42,038 --> 00:10:44,130
and let the public outcry do the work.

168
00:10:44,928 --> 00:10:46,409
Shareholders gave in.

169
00:10:47,207 --> 00:10:53,883
By 1869, he had merged them into one giant
line running from New York City toward the
Great Lakes.

170
00:10:54,681 --> 00:10:57,307
The Commodore now owned the road to the
West.

171
00:10:58,105 --> 00:10:59,468
And he wanted all of it.

172
00:11:00,414 --> 00:11:05,097
To reach Chicago and the markets of the
West, Vanderbilt needed one more line.

173
00:11:05,898 --> 00:11:07,196
The Erie Railway.

174
00:11:07,994 --> 00:11:13,394
It was a rickety, badly run railroad,
controlled by a trio of Wall Street
operators:

175
00:11:13,580 --> 00:11:17,808
Daniel Drew, and two younger men, Jay
Gould and Jim Fisk.

176
00:11:18,606 --> 00:11:23,485
In 1868, Vanderbilt started buying Erie
stock to take control.

177
00:11:24,283 --> 00:11:25,712
It should have been simple.

178
00:11:26,510 --> 00:11:27,886
He had done it before.

179
00:11:28,684 --> 00:11:30,399
But these three had a trick.

180
00:11:31,197 --> 00:11:34,488
They were allowed to convert company bonds
into new shares.

181
00:11:35,286 --> 00:11:39,214
So every time Vanderbilt bought more
stock, they printed more.

182
00:11:40,013 --> 00:11:43,172
Thousands of new certificates, flooding
the market.

183
00:11:43,971 --> 00:11:45,087
He bought them all.

184
00:11:45,885 --> 00:11:47,196
And they kept printing.

185
00:11:47,994 --> 00:11:49,475
Vanderbilt went to court.

186
00:11:50,273 --> 00:11:52,652
A judge ordered the three men to stop.

187
00:11:53,450 --> 00:11:56,076
They found another judge who ordered the
opposite.

188
00:11:56,875 --> 00:12:00,764
Two courts, two orders, one railroad in
chaos.

189
00:12:01,562 --> 00:12:03,837
Then Vanderbilt got arrest warrants.

190
00:12:04,635 --> 00:12:10,373
Gould, Fisk and Drew grabbed millions in
cash and fled across the Hudson River to
Jersey City,

191
00:12:10,572 --> 00:12:15,842
where, the story goes, they holed up in a
hotel with armed guards and even cannons.

192
00:12:16,640 --> 00:12:20,972
From there, Gould went to the state
capital in Albany, with a bag of money.

193
00:12:21,770 --> 00:12:24,878
And he made sure the legislature approved
the new stock.

194
00:12:25,677 --> 00:12:27,561
The Commodore had been outplayed.

195
00:12:28,359 --> 00:12:30,087
Not by a better businessman.

196
00:12:30,885 --> 00:12:32,144
By a better crook.

197
00:12:32,942 --> 00:12:34,761
Vanderbilt did not get Erie.

198
00:12:35,559 --> 00:12:38,563
He settled, took a loss, and walked away.

199
00:12:39,362 --> 00:12:42,652
It was one of the very few times in his
life that he lost.

200
00:12:43,450 --> 00:12:46,102
At seventy-four, he could have called it a
day.

201
00:12:46,901 --> 00:12:50,034
But Cornelius Vanderbilt did not know how
to be beaten.

202
00:12:50,833 --> 00:12:53,589
He only knew how to turn a loss into a
lesson.

203
00:12:54,388 --> 00:12:57,951
And he went back to the one thing he knew
he could do better than anyone.

204
00:12:58,878 --> 00:13:00,710
Vanderbilt went back to building.

205
00:13:01,510 --> 00:13:03,837
He took the New York Central and fixed it.

206
00:13:04,635 --> 00:13:07,222
He replaced weak iron rails with steel.

207
00:13:08,021 --> 00:13:09,735
He rebuilt old bridges.

208
00:13:10,534 --> 00:13:16,806
He added more tracks, so freight and
passengers could move faster and safer
than on any competing line.

209
00:13:17,604 --> 00:13:22,170
And he kept the fares low enough that
shippers had no reason to go anywhere
else.

210
00:13:22,969 --> 00:13:30,673
Then he extended the empire west toward
Buffalo, and onward to Chicago, linking
the cities of the Midwest to the port of
New York.

211
00:13:31,471 --> 00:13:35,139
In 1871, he opened a monument to himself.

212
00:13:35,938 --> 00:13:44,228
Grand Central Depot, on Forty-Second
Street, a vast terminal of iron and glass,
on the same ground where Grand Central
stands today.

213
00:13:45,026 --> 00:13:48,655
A statue of the Commodore would later look
down over the trains.

214
00:13:49,453 --> 00:13:52,626
He also did what every great tycoon of his
age did.

215
00:13:53,425 --> 00:13:59,345
He issued new stock, tens of millions of
dollars of it, far more than the
railroad's real value.

216
00:14:00,143 --> 00:14:02,405
His critics called it watering the stock.

217
00:14:03,203 --> 00:14:05,269
Vanderbilt called it good business.

218
00:14:06,068 --> 00:14:07,613
The money kept coming in.

219
00:14:08,411 --> 00:14:11,415
Meanwhile, his private life was getting
strange.

220
00:14:12,214 --> 00:14:18,251
After his first wife died, he married a
young woman from Alabama, more than forty
years younger than him.

221
00:14:19,050 --> 00:14:24,618
He began consulting mediums for advice on
his health and his investments, including
two sisters,

222
00:14:24,766 --> 00:14:30,673
Victoria Woodhull and Tennessee Claflin,
who became the first women to open a
brokerage on Wall Street.

223
00:14:31,471 --> 00:14:33,511
His own family was horrified.

224
00:14:34,310 --> 00:14:36,845
They thought the old man was losing his
mind.

225
00:14:37,643 --> 00:14:39,775
But his empire never slowed down.

226
00:14:40,573 --> 00:14:47,574
When the Panic of 1873 wiped out banks and
railroads across America, the Vanderbilt
lines survived.

227
00:14:48,372 --> 00:14:52,327
By the mid-1870s, he was the richest man
in the country.

228
00:14:53,125 --> 00:14:56,324
And he was not done teaching his family
one last lesson.

229
00:14:57,246 --> 00:15:03,284
Cornelius Vanderbilt died on January
fourth, 1877, at the age of eighty-two.

230
00:15:04,084 --> 00:15:06,294
The whole city stopped for his funeral.

231
00:15:07,092 --> 00:15:10,460
His estate was valued at about one hundred
million dollars.

232
00:15:11,259 --> 00:15:15,773
By some accounts, that was more than the
cash held by the United States Treasury.

233
00:15:16,571 --> 00:15:18,025
Then the will was read.

234
00:15:18,824 --> 00:15:22,570
Vanderbilt had done something almost no
rich man did at the time.

235
00:15:23,368 --> 00:15:26,228
He did not divide his empire among his
children.

236
00:15:27,027 --> 00:15:31,827
He gave nearly all of it, about ninety-
five million dollars, to one son.

237
00:15:32,626 --> 00:15:33,715
William Henry.

238
00:15:34,514 --> 00:15:38,611
The rest got small shares, or trust funds
with strings attached.

239
00:15:39,410 --> 00:15:43,520
His other son, Cornelius Jeremiah, got a
limited trust.

240
00:15:44,319 --> 00:15:49,549
He sued, claiming his father had been
insane, pointing to the mediums and the
spirits.

241
00:15:50,347 --> 00:15:53,468
The court threw the case out in 1879.

242
00:15:54,266 --> 00:15:56,567
And here is the part nobody expected.

243
00:15:57,365 --> 00:16:00,851
William Henry was the son the Commodore
had once called a fool.

244
00:16:01,649 --> 00:16:03,377
He had sent him to run a farm.

245
00:16:04,175 --> 00:16:08,520
But in the eight years after his father
died, William Henry doubled the fortune,

246
00:16:08,707 --> 00:16:13,090
to roughly two hundred million dollars,
and became the richest man in the world.

247
00:16:13,889 --> 00:16:16,268
Then he died in 1885.

248
00:16:17,066 --> 00:16:19,106
And that is where the story turns.

249
00:16:19,904 --> 00:16:24,145
His children and grandchildren built a
Gilded Age dynasty of palaces.

250
00:16:24,944 --> 00:16:26,437
The Breakers in Newport.

251
00:16:27,235 --> 00:16:31,228
Biltmore in North Carolina, the largest
private home in America.

252
00:16:32,027 --> 00:16:34,900
Fifth Avenue mansions, one after another.

253
00:16:35,699 --> 00:16:37,609
None of them had to build anything.

254
00:16:38,407 --> 00:16:39,913
They only had to spend.

255
00:16:40,712 --> 00:16:46,020
The great fortune was split again and
again, and eaten by taxes, by lavish
living,

256
00:16:46,141 --> 00:16:47,322
and by bad luck.

257
00:16:48,121 --> 00:16:51,632
The railroad empire itself collapsed in
the twentieth century.

258
00:16:52,431 --> 00:16:57,752
The New York Central merged into the
failing Penn Central, which went bankrupt
in 1970.

259
00:16:58,550 --> 00:17:01,671
The mansions were torn down or turned into
museums.

260
00:17:02,470 --> 00:17:06,098
And in 1973, the family held a reunion.

261
00:17:06,897 --> 00:17:12,062
About one hundred and twenty Vanderbilt
descendants gathered at Vanderbilt
University in Nashville.

262
00:17:12,860 --> 00:17:16,567
Not a single one of them, it was reported,
was a millionaire.

263
00:17:17,365 --> 00:17:21,255
The richest family in America had spent
its way back to the middle class,

264
00:17:21,441 --> 00:17:23,090
in less than a hundred years.

265
00:17:24,030 --> 00:17:26,799
So what did Cornelius Vanderbilt really
build?

266
00:17:27,600 --> 00:17:28,898
Not steamboats.

267
00:17:29,696 --> 00:17:30,968
Not railroads.

268
00:17:31,767 --> 00:17:32,869
Not even money.

269
00:17:33,668 --> 00:17:38,520
His real genius was seeing where the world
was going to move next, and getting there
first.

270
00:17:39,319 --> 00:17:42,049
When water was the road, he owned the
water.

271
00:17:42,847 --> 00:17:46,437
When the road moved to iron and steel, he
owned the rails.

272
00:17:47,235 --> 00:17:49,731
And each time, he won the same way.

273
00:17:50,530 --> 00:17:54,302
Lower prices, better service, and a
refusal to quit.

274
00:17:55,100 --> 00:17:58,143
But he made one mistake that no victory
could fix.

275
00:17:58,941 --> 00:18:01,762
He built a fortune that needed a
Vanderbilt to run it.

276
00:18:02,561 --> 00:18:05,773
And he never taught the next generations
to be Vanderbilts.

277
00:18:06,571 --> 00:18:08,624
Only two things of his survived.

278
00:18:09,423 --> 00:18:16,710
A university in Nashville, funded by a one
million dollar gift in 1873, that still
carries his name.

279
00:18:17,509 --> 00:18:19,588
And a train station in New York.

280
00:18:20,386 --> 00:18:24,367
The Commodore did not lose his money to a
rival, or to the government.

281
00:18:25,165 --> 00:18:28,169
He lost it to the one enemy he never
thought to fight.

282
00:18:28,967 --> 00:18:29,614
Time.

283
00:18:30,399 --> 00:18:35,773
If you enjoyed this story, subscribe, and
tell me in the comments which fortune
should be next.

284
00:18:36,571 --> 00:18:38,781
Because every empire has a story.

285
00:18:39,579 --> 00:18:41,567
And most of them end the same way.

